\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.13}{\ignorespaces \textbf {Short-run}: A combined ISO minimizes dispatch costs subject to transmission constraints (engineering problem). \textbf {Long-run}: Generators choose capacity to maximize profits given expected prices (equilibrium).}}{43}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.14}{\ignorespaces Static Effects of Transmission Expansion: Price Convergence and Spatial Arbitrage. In the low-cost (exporting) region, prices rise and producer surplus increases (red shaded area). In the high-cost (importing) region, prices fall and consumer surplus increases (blue shaded area).}}{43}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.15}{\ignorespaces Dynamic Effects of Transmission Expansion: The Investment Signal. Expanded market access shifts the marginal benefit curve outward for generators in resource-rich regions, leading to increased equilibrium capacity investment.}}{44}{figure.caption.36}%
